1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,359
A small garden today. Mint, basil, and a few
radishes.

2
00:00:04,609 --> 00:00:06,280
Small garden. Ambitious gardener.

3
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:09,660
Then let us give the gardener a plan.

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:14,499
How small are we talking? Can my plan fit on
the bench?

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:17,900
Mint? Are we growing money?

6
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:21,410
That would be a very useful garden.

7
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:24,490
Our shopping list might get smaller.

8
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:29,290
Would we have to water the coins to make
change?

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,707
This mint is an herb. The other mint makes
coins.

10
00:00:34,957 --> 00:00:36,778
Two jobs, one word.

11
00:00:37,028 --> 00:00:40,042
And only one belongs in our garden.

12
00:00:40,292 --> 00:00:44,499
Good. I'd rather smell an herb than smell my
pocket.

13
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,468
Mint left, basil in the middle, radishes on
the right.

14
00:00:49,718 --> 00:00:51,436
Three pots. Three saucers.

15
00:00:51,686 --> 00:00:54,529
And three labels that tell the truth.

16
00:00:54,779 --> 00:00:59,498
Good. We'll be able to tell the seed pot from
the rest.

17
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,550
My brother Ben is bringing the pots.

18
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,484
Empty ones?

19
00:01:05,734 --> 00:01:09,991
Yes. We are supplying the plants, not ordering
a forest.

20
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:14,498
Three is plenty. I haven't even met the mint
yet.

21
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,900
Labels and gloves are ready.

22
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,980
Do the seeds need tiny gloves?

23
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:23,630
No. Just our careful hands.

24
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:28,860
Mine are bigger than a seed. I can lend a
hand.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,719
I will bring their drink.

26
00:01:32,969 --> 00:01:37,176
Bring the can first. We will check the soil
outside.

27
00:01:37,426 --> 00:01:40,042
No straws for the basil, then.

28
00:01:40,292 --> 00:01:44,499
Good. I'll wait to see what the soil actually
needs.

29
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:49,611
Stay on the clear path with your hands free of
soil.

30
00:01:49,861 --> 00:01:52,552
Even my grand plan is walking carefully.

31
00:01:52,802 --> 00:01:55,138
That is a good first step.

32
00:01:55,388 --> 00:01:59,499
Keep your eyes on the path till we reach the
bench.

33
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,860
Three empty homes, as promised.

34
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:06,316
Excellent. Their new residents are already
waiting.

35
00:02:06,566 --> 00:02:09,772
Hello, Ben. Thank you for bringing them.

36
00:02:10,022 --> 00:02:14,497
You're welcome. I was careful to bring their
saucers too.

37
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:18,911
Three pots and three saucers. Nothing growing
yet.

38
00:02:19,161 --> 00:02:21,380
Not even a surprise tree.

39
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:24,246
That keeps our small plan small.

40
00:02:24,496 --> 00:02:29,498
I think we can give these three all the
attention they need.

41
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,330
We nearly ordered a money plant.

42
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:36,840
Dan once labeled an empty box “treasure.”

43
00:02:37,090 --> 00:02:39,490
It contained very valuable possibilities.

44
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:44,290
I bet that box didn't give you much change,
Dad.

45
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,343
Enjoy your garden. Label first, guess later.

46
00:02:48,593 --> 00:02:51,061
A useful family motto. Thanks, Ben.

47
00:02:51,311 --> 00:02:54,529
We will show you the journal next time.

48
00:02:54,779 --> 00:02:59,498
I'll save you the part about what kind of mint
it is.

49
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:03,607
There is a hole in the bottom.

50
00:03:03,857 --> 00:03:06,554
Extra water needs a way out.

51
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:09,911
A pot with an exit. Sensible architecture.

52
00:03:10,161 --> 00:03:14,498
So the saucer belongs under it before we add
anything.

53
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,283
Five hundred milliliters in the can.

54
00:03:18,533 --> 00:03:20,470
That sounds satisfyingly official.

55
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:25,618
It is a record, not an instruction to use it
all.

56
00:03:25,868 --> 00:03:29,497
Good. I was picturing one very thirsty pot.

57
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,719
Those two already have leaves.

58
00:03:32,969 --> 00:03:36,778
They are young plants. The radishes start as
seeds.

59
00:03:37,028 --> 00:03:39,644
Different beginnings, the same careful
attention.

60
00:03:39,894 --> 00:03:44,498
I can see the difference even before the
labels go in.

61
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,900
Tools here. Clear path there.

62
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:50,550
And nobody swings the shovel.

63
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,770
Especially our ambitious gardener.

64
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:57,140
Understood. Even ambitious gardeners carry
their tools down low.

65
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,198
Left for mint, middle for basil, right for
radishes.

66
00:04:04,448 --> 00:04:06,988
Each pot gets its own saucer.

67
00:04:07,238 --> 00:04:10,164
Now the bench has a clear plan.

68
00:04:10,414 --> 00:04:14,498
Good. I can find the middle without turning
anything around.

69
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,470
Can my coin help?

70
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,410
It can remind us which mint we mean.

71
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:24,490
A very profitable piece of paper.

72
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:29,290
This coin gets to stay dry in the notebook,
right?

73
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:32,836
Gloves on, hands away from mouths.

74
00:04:33,086 --> 00:04:35,777
We will wash properly when we finish.

75
00:04:36,027 --> 00:04:39,783
The plants are not the only things getting
good care.

76
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:44,499
Good. We can keep the dirt on the bench where
it belongs.

77
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,116
It is already a little damp.

78
00:04:48,366 --> 00:04:51,380
Yes. We will check before adding water.

79
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:54,644
Slowly keeps the soil where it belongs.

80
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:59,498
Let's keep a little space at the rim as we go.

81
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:02,860
Leave room at the top?

82
00:05:03,110 --> 00:05:06,316
Yes. Room for water without spilling soil.

83
00:05:06,566 --> 00:05:10,195
Even a pot appreciates a little breathing
space.

84
00:05:10,445 --> 00:05:14,497
So the soil stays inside when the water
arrives.

85
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,654
One generous drink for everyone?

86
00:05:17,904 --> 00:05:21,602
Check first. Damp soil does not need a flood.

87
00:05:21,852 --> 00:05:25,550
Let us not give the roots a swimming pool.

88
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:29,498
All right. I'll put the big splash on hold.

89
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:33,197
It looks much greener than money.

90
00:05:33,447 --> 00:05:36,144
Today we are planting, not tasting.

91
00:05:36,394 --> 00:05:39,501
Its leaves are the better clue anyway.

92
00:05:39,751 --> 00:05:44,498
I'll use my eyes, then. Those leaves have a
pretty shape.

93
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,197
The tag says mint. Left pot.

94
00:05:48,447 --> 00:05:51,144
I checked the label before guessing.

95
00:05:51,394 --> 00:05:54,501
A strong start for our ambitious gardener.

96
00:05:54,751 --> 00:05:59,498
And a better start than planting a coin in
that pot.

97
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:04,427
You are holding the roots, not tugging the
leaves.

98
00:06:04,677 --> 00:06:06,554
Exactly. Gentle hands underneath.

99
00:06:06,804 --> 00:06:09,911
No plant gets pulled by its hairstyle.

100
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:14,498
The little leaves hardly moved when the roots
came out.

101
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,470
It has moved house.

102
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:19,690
Without changing its name.

103
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:23,200
Now we support the roots with soil.

104
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:28,430
Good. It can get comfortable before we give it
a drink.

105
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:32,900
Like tucking in a blanket?

106
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:35,980
Gently. The roots still need air.

107
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:38,030
Comfortable, not squashed.

108
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:42,400
I can leave a little space around the stem.

109
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:49,093
One empty nursery pot, ready to use another
day.

110
00:06:49,343 --> 00:06:52,936
So the plant has not left a twin behind.

111
00:06:53,186 --> 00:06:54,904
Only its old address.

112
00:06:55,154 --> 00:06:59,497
A house with nobody in it is much easier to
stack.

113
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,470
The leaves look different.

114
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,550
This is basil. Its tag agrees.

115
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,630
Middle pot, just as we planned.

116
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:13,430
Two plants and two different shapes. I see it
now.

117
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,300
Another moving day.

118
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,950
And the same gentle lift.

119
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,600
Our garden has excellent movers.

120
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:27,400
Good. That stem still looks the same after the
move.

121
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,377
Basil in the middle.

122
00:07:32,627 --> 00:07:35,734
Soil around the roots, below the rim.

123
00:07:35,984 --> 00:07:39,091
I have its label ready for later.

124
00:07:39,341 --> 00:07:44,498
Keep it with us. We'll do the names together
in a bit.

125
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:48,116
Not too tight around the stem.

126
00:07:48,366 --> 00:07:51,380
That is right. Support it without squeezing.

127
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:54,644
A comfortable fit, even without shoe sizes.

128
00:07:54,894 --> 00:07:59,498
I think that stem would vote for a little
elbow room.

129
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:02,836
Which plant is in the middle?

130
00:08:03,086 --> 00:08:06,842
Basil. The mint is not making change on the
left.

131
00:08:07,092 --> 00:08:09,783
Good. Our garden names are staying put.

132
00:08:10,033 --> 00:08:14,499
So the one on the left is mint. I can remember
that.

133
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:17,900
This pot looks rather empty.

134
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:20,550
It is ready, not forgotten.

135
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,200
Its residents are much smaller.

136
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:28,000
How small? I can hardly see them from this
side.

137
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,593
Three seeds for this pot.

138
00:08:32,843 --> 00:08:35,686
The rest stay dry in their packet.

139
00:08:35,936 --> 00:08:39,529
A tiny beginning does not need a crowded home.

140
00:08:39,779 --> 00:08:44,498
Good. I'll watch all three spaces on top
before we cover them.

141
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,706
Space between them, and not too deep.

142
00:08:48,956 --> 00:08:52,585
This packet tells us what these seeds need.

143
00:08:52,835 --> 00:08:54,772
No basement apartments, then.

144
00:08:55,022 --> 00:08:59,497
I'll use what the packet says for these
particular seeds.

145
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:02,470
One in the back.

146
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:04,520
Two still waiting.

147
00:09:04,770 --> 00:09:08,030
They will all have their own space.

148
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:12,830
Can I keep count while the other two are
waiting?

149
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,470
One for this space.

150
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:19,690
A very orderly neighborhood.

151
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:22,770
And one neighbor still to come.

152
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:28,000
Good. The last little neighbor has a spot of
its own.

153
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,900
The last neighbor has arrived.

154
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:35,550
And the dish is empty.

155
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:39,060
Now we can cover all three gently.

156
00:09:39,310 --> 00:09:44,290
Three in their places. Now we can leave them
in peace.

157
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,437
Where are the radishes?

158
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:50,470
Below the soil. They need time.

159
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:54,772
Then our first picture can be a quiet pot.

160
00:09:55,022 --> 00:09:59,497
Okay. We can look without asking them to hurry
up.

161
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,129
Label first, guess later. Ben had a point.

162
00:10:04,379 --> 00:10:06,316
Three names, three stakes.

163
00:10:06,566 --> 00:10:09,772
Let the handwriting agree with the plants.

164
00:10:10,022 --> 00:10:14,497
I'll read the names with you before they go
in.

165
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,470
Mint. The leafy kind.

166
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:20,550
That label will help us remember.

167
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:24,060
My wallet can stop expecting a harvest.

168
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:28,860
Good. It would be a long wait for pocket
money.

169
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,470
Basil in the middle.

170
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,690
Name and place match.

171
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:38,200
And the original tag agrees with both.

172
00:10:38,450 --> 00:10:43,860
Good. I'd like to recognize the leaves as well
as the name.

173
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,646
The name is taller than the plant.

174
00:10:48,896 --> 00:10:50,633
For today.

175
00:10:50,883 --> 00:10:54,443
It will remind us what is growing underneath.

176
00:10:54,693 --> 00:10:59,498
Then I won't think the bare soil means we
forgot one.

177
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,607
Day one: planted. No radish shoots yet.

178
00:11:03,857 --> 00:11:06,964
An honest report from a quiet pot.

179
00:11:07,214 --> 00:11:10,321
That is how a useful journal begins.

180
00:11:10,571 --> 00:11:14,498
I'll leave a little room for whatever happens
next.

181
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,343
Perhaps the mint wants the whole can.

182
00:11:18,593 --> 00:11:22,186
Let us ask the soil before making that offer.

183
00:11:22,436 --> 00:11:25,279
It has already declined the swimming pool.

184
00:11:25,529 --> 00:11:29,497
Good thing we checked. My first offer was
rather large.

185
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:34,017
Start with one small measured amount, then
observe.

186
00:11:34,267 --> 00:11:36,144
One hundred, not everything.

187
00:11:36,394 --> 00:11:39,501
Now the can has four hundred left.

188
00:11:39,751 --> 00:11:44,498
I'll watch the soil while the rest stays in
the can.

189
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:48,319
A small drink, around the roots.

190
00:11:48,569 --> 00:11:50,362
No swimming lessons.

191
00:11:50,612 --> 00:11:53,859
Now we watch what the water does.

192
00:11:54,109 --> 00:11:59,498
I can wait. The water doesn't need my help to
sink in.

193
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,040
A little came through the bottom.

194
00:12:03,290 --> 00:12:06,988
The soil has settled. That is enough for now.

195
00:12:07,238 --> 00:12:09,778
Stopping is part of watering too.

196
00:12:10,028 --> 00:12:14,498
Yes. Let's leave that plant in peace and check
the next.

197
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:19,256
Three hundred in the can, one hundred in the
cup.

198
00:12:19,506 --> 00:12:22,552
Our water is getting a very careful
accountant.

199
00:12:22,802 --> 00:12:25,493
And the basil gets a careful drink.

200
00:12:25,743 --> 00:12:29,499
Good. The cup's easier to control than the
whole can.

201
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:32,593
Same small measure, different plant.

202
00:12:32,843 --> 00:12:35,311
We still check its own soil.

203
00:12:35,561 --> 00:12:39,904
The amount is our record, not a rule for every
day.

204
00:12:40,154 --> 00:12:44,497
So we write what happened today and check
tomorrow for ourselves.

205
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:48,812
Do all pots want the same drink forever?

206
00:12:49,062 --> 00:12:52,760
No. Weather, roots, soil and pot size change
that.

207
00:12:53,010 --> 00:12:55,550
We will check again another day.

208
00:12:55,800 --> 00:12:59,498
Okay. The pots can have different answers next
time.

209
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:03,607
One hundred for the seed pot, gently.

210
00:13:03,857 --> 00:13:07,784
A slow pour will keep the soil in place.

211
00:13:08,034 --> 00:13:10,321
The tiny neighbors dislike waterfalls.

212
00:13:10,571 --> 00:13:14,498
Point taken. I'll keep the pour gentle and
close.

213
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,134
Nothing green yet.

214
00:13:17,384 --> 00:13:21,125
That is normal. We planted seeds, not a
shortcut.

215
00:13:21,375 --> 00:13:24,335
Our journal can wait for real changes.

216
00:13:24,585 --> 00:13:29,498
Good. I want to see the very first bit of
green myself.

217
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:34,468
Two hundred left. I do not have to spend it.

218
00:13:34,718 --> 00:13:37,186
Exactly. Use what the plants need.

219
00:13:37,436 --> 00:13:40,279
Three small drinks, not one grand flood.

220
00:13:40,529 --> 00:13:44,497
Good. Leaving water in the can feels like
helping now.

221
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,231
Three hundred used. Two hundred left.

222
00:13:48,481 --> 00:13:50,218
Together, five hundred.

223
00:13:50,468 --> 00:13:54,858
And tomorrow we check the soil before choosing
any amount.

224
00:13:55,108 --> 00:13:59,498
I'll write the amount we used, so we remember
today.

225
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,900
No pools under the houses.

226
00:14:03,150 --> 00:14:06,410
Roots need drainage, not a permanent bath.

227
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:09,490
The swimming club is officially closed.

228
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:13,860
Good. These little houses won't need lifeboats
after all.

229
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,968
The tree makes shade over there.

230
00:14:18,218 --> 00:14:20,686
These tags ask for good light.

231
00:14:20,936 --> 00:14:25,279
This sunny strip gives us room to check the
pots safely.

232
00:14:25,529 --> 00:14:29,497
We can see all the labels from the path there.

233
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,470
Left pot, left place.

234
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:35,120
The label faces the path.

235
00:14:35,370 --> 00:14:38,200
And the walking space stays clear.

236
00:14:38,450 --> 00:14:43,860
Good. We'll be able to read it without
stepping off the path.

237
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:47,787
Basil in the middle again.

238
00:14:48,037 --> 00:14:50,324
It has kept its address.

239
00:14:50,574 --> 00:14:54,501
And both of you have kept the path clear.

240
00:14:54,751 --> 00:14:59,498
Good. We'll still know which one is basil from
over here.

241
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:02,377
Radishes on the right.

242
00:15:02,627 --> 00:15:05,734
A quiet home, with a clear name.

243
00:15:05,984 --> 00:15:09,911
Now we can watch without digging up the
answer.

244
00:15:10,161 --> 00:15:14,498
I can wait. The label's got today's answer on
it.

245
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:18,391
Two leafy plants and one pot of waiting.

246
00:15:18,641 --> 00:15:21,195
The seed positions go in the diagram.

247
00:15:21,445 --> 00:15:25,684
A record can show what we know and what we can
see.

248
00:15:25,934 --> 00:15:29,499
Then let's draw the soil just the way it
looks.

249
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:33,546
Can the seeds stay out of the rain?

250
00:15:33,796 --> 00:15:36,842
The spare packet goes on the dry shelf.

251
00:15:37,092 --> 00:15:39,783
Our tools get a home as well.

252
00:15:40,033 --> 00:15:44,499
Good. We won't mix up the paper seeds and the
planted ones.

253
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:48,197
Two hundred saved, not secretly added.

254
00:15:48,447 --> 00:15:50,734
My grand gesture can wait.

255
00:15:50,984 --> 00:15:54,501
Today your careful stopping was the useful
part.

256
00:15:54,751 --> 00:15:59,498
Good. Tomorrow the soil can tell us what to do
next.

257
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,218
Gloves stay out here.

258
00:16:02,468 --> 00:16:04,936
Yes. We still wash our hands.

259
00:16:05,186 --> 00:16:09,905
No soil goes on the door handle if we can help
it.

260
00:16:10,155 --> 00:16:14,498
I'll leave my gloves here and go with you to
wash.

261
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,330
Before a snack, a proper wash.

262
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,550
Even the gardener’s accountant?

263
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:24,060
Especially anyone with soil on their hands.

264
00:16:24,310 --> 00:16:29,290
Especially me. I handled the pots as well as
the numbers.

265
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,470
Day one is complete.

266
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:36,410
And the paper coin is still above ground.

267
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:39,490
Exactly where a pretend coin belongs.

268
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:44,290
Good. My pencil doesn't have to grow a radish
today.

269
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:48,426
Will the radishes be ready after lunch?

270
00:16:48,676 --> 00:16:51,988
No. We will watch over the coming days.

271
00:16:52,238 --> 00:16:55,164
Then patience belongs on the supply list.

272
00:16:55,414 --> 00:16:59,498
Can we still look if there's nothing new to
see?

273
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:03,343
I will leave room for what changes.

274
00:17:03,593 --> 00:17:06,061
We cannot write the answer yet.

275
00:17:06,311 --> 00:17:10,279
That is a good reason to come back and look.

276
00:17:10,529 --> 00:17:14,497
I'll leave a blank spot for the first green
bit.

277
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:19,604
Your paper coin grew into a very good joke.

278
00:17:19,854 --> 00:17:21,647
And a reminder.

279
00:17:21,897 --> 00:17:23,859
Both are worth keeping.

280
00:17:24,109 --> 00:17:29,498
And the only thing we had to spend was a
little time.

281
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:33,330
A last look from the path.

282
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:36,410
No extra drink, no extra seed.

283
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:39,490
Just the garden we actually planted.

284
00:17:39,740 --> 00:17:44,290
I'll wave from here. The seeds don't need
digging up.

285
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:47,719
I nearly wanted a forest.

286
00:17:47,969 --> 00:17:51,380
Three pots give us room to notice things.

287
00:17:51,630 --> 00:17:54,644
And time to care for them together.

288
00:17:54,894 --> 00:17:59,498
And I already know all three of these
neighbors by name.

289
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,116
We will visit again, tiny neighbors.

290
00:18:03,366 --> 00:18:05,982
Quiet neighbors are easy to hear.

291
00:18:06,232 --> 00:18:10,041
Especially when Dad is not giving a gardening
speech.

292
00:18:10,291 --> 00:18:14,498
They can have a quiet afternoon. We did enough
today.

293
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:19,584
I wanted to be helpful. Checking helped more
than pouring.

294
00:18:19,834 --> 00:18:22,374
You listened and changed the plan.

295
00:18:22,624 --> 00:18:24,778
The roots probably appreciate that.

296
00:18:25,028 --> 00:18:29,498
Good. It's the first time I've felt proud of
leftover water.

297
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:34,017
Labels, a drawing, a water record. Everyone
helped.

298
00:18:34,267 --> 00:18:37,784
Even a quiet pot has a story now.

299
00:18:38,034 --> 00:18:40,321
A very short first chapter.

300
00:18:40,571 --> 00:18:44,498
Day one is enough. The rest hasn't happened
yet.

301
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:49,359
We can tell everyone about our garden at the
gathering.

302
00:18:49,609 --> 00:18:52,009
A true story. No money harvest.

303
00:18:52,259 --> 00:18:55,389
And no promise that the radishes will hurry.

304
00:18:55,639 --> 00:18:59,498
I'll tell them who helped instead of promising
a harvest.

305
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:03,952
Our garden is not bigger than the house.

306
00:19:04,202 --> 00:19:05,886
A wise limit.

307
00:19:06,136 --> 00:19:09,991
It still has enough names to keep you busy.

308
00:19:10,241 --> 00:19:14,498
Good. The only thing that got taller was Dad's
plan.

309
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:18,760
Herbs upright. Radish soil bare. Saucers
empty.

310
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:20,980
A surprisingly satisfying report.

311
00:19:21,230 --> 00:19:24,060
Because it says what really happened.

312
00:19:24,310 --> 00:19:28,860
Good. I'll leave the page about tomorrow blank
for now.

313
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:33,607
Three pots, six helpers, one better plan.

314
00:19:33,857 --> 00:19:36,144
And a morning well spent.

315
00:19:36,394 --> 00:19:39,501
The mint is still not making change.

316
00:19:39,751 --> 00:19:44,498
But the leaves smell better than the inside of
a wallet.

317
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:49,190
We will come back and see what changes.

318
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:51,240
Together.

319
00:19:51,490 --> 00:19:53,460
I can wait. Mostly.

320
00:19:53,710 --> 00:19:59,120
We'll see it together when it happens. That
part's worth waiting for.
